  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Be the duck",
      "skill": "Sequences",
      "body": "Tape a three by three grid on the floor, 30 cm squares. One child is the duck and stands on the start square, facing the way the challenge says. The class lays the cards in a row and reads them aloud one at a time, and the duck does exactly what each card says, nothing more.",
      "ask": "Did the duck end up where we meant? Which card took it somewhere else?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Which way is right?",
      "skill": "Left and right",
      "body": "Put a sticker on every child's right hand. Before a Turn card goes down, everyone turns their own body the way the duck would. A Turn card turns the duck where it stands; it does not move it to a new square.",
      "ask": "When the duck is facing you, is its right the same as your right?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Count like Tam",
      "skill": "Counting",
      "body": "In chapter 1 of The Duck Who Stayed, Tam counts things. Before any card goes down, point and count the squares from the duck to the barn, out loud. Lay that many Walk forward cards. Then act it out and count the steps again.",
      "ask": "How did you know how many Walk cards you needed?"
    },
    {
      "title": "The last card",
      "skill": "Endings",
      "body": "Count to Moo-Bot ends with Hello, and Corn in the feed lane ends with Peck the ground. Those cards go last, after the duck has arrived. The duck stands still for them, and each one counts as a step.",
      "ask": "What happens if Peck the ground comes before the duck gets to the corn?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Real robots on a farm",
      "skill": "Robots and people",
      "body": "Some real farms have robots. A milking robot stays in one place, and the cows walk to it. Our duck only plays a game about farm robots: it does not milk, carry feed or look after animals. Ask the class to name farm jobs and say who does each one.",
      "ask": "Which farm jobs need a person?"
    }
  ],
  "yours": []
}
